[[00:10:50]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=650s) 12, the plan specifically calls out the Reed Avenue corridor as part of developing the west edge of downtown. That same section also highlights improving downtown infrastructure and appearance as a priority because those investments shape how residents and visitors experience Cheyenne.

[[00:11:06]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=666s) Third, a stronger and more vibrant downtown attracts continued private investment. We've seen a lot of private investment on the West Edge already. Public infrastructure is often what gives the private sector the confidence to move forward. When we improve access, appearance, and connectivity, we create conditions for more retail, lodging, dining, housing, and other business development. Here at Visit Cheyenne, we

[[00:11:27]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=687s) believe downtown matters because it's one of the clearest expressions of our community to visitors and residents alike. Reed Avenue is not just a corridor project. It's a signal about where Cheyenne is heading. Thank you for your consideration. >> Well said, Jim. Thank you. Anybody else?

