[[00:37:17]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=2237s) There are likely endangered plant species on this property along with bald eagle and raptor nests which are federally protected under the bald and golden eagle protection act and the migratory bird treaty act. These laws make it illegal to disturb nests interfere with eggs and feeding or raising young. There's also surface low surface flow from Crow Creek on this parcel which is an integral part of Cheyenne's water supply including our drinking water. Even when it appears to be dry, there is still water moving below the surface that helps recharge the local aquifers. Adding a data center there will reduce recharge and increase pollutant loading. The impact

[[00:37:47]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=2267s) of disrupting this fragile system will extend beyond this property, weakening the property rights argument. With all due respect to the Florida based property owner, the Colorado based developers, and the Texas-based operators, Wyoming water rights, our health, quality of life, cost of living, and the long-term livability of our community must come before opportunistic get-rich schemes. Another concern is

[[00:38:09]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=2289s) that this proposal follows a recurring pattern of secrecy that prioritizes the interest of the joint powers board chem leads and their dual overlapping and private members over the public's right to transparent governance. When representatives from Meta, Microsoft, Cruso related digital and tall grass pay for seats on the board of leads which according to their own bylaws guarantees influence on private economic development decisions. The public loses its voice. When Leeds is paid by the city as the official contracted economic developer while also acting as the private land owner and manage sites they recommend for development, meaning they stand to profit or otherwise benefit from their recommendations,

[[00:38:43]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=2323s) an inherent conflict is created. When the mayor and county commissioners serve on these same boards and are involved in the same private decisions, then participate in related public votes. It raises serious questions about objectivity. This council had the opportunity to fix this in 2023, but chose not to by voting down an ethics ordinance requiring disclosure and recusals in such situations. Cheyenne deserves an economic development model where our elected officials make decisions in the open, not behind the closed doors of a private nonprofit boardroom where code names like Project Cosmo are created, non-disclosure agreements are signed, and shell companies like Goat Systems LLC are developed to

[[00:39:18]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9vnXtjZpR0&t=2358s) hide obscure details from the public. While projects are fasttracked on land owned by leads, the public services committee rightfully voted against this annexation partly because of the impact on our water supply and partly because the data center component was withheld from the public. I urge you to follow that recommendation and vote no, not only on this annexation but all future annexations and resonings for the purpose of data centers or business parks until robust ethics and disclosure standards are in place. Our elected officials elected officials should not be serving two masters. Thank you.

